# 13. Experimentation plan

Each experiment should specify:

- Hypothesis;
- Randomization unit;
- Treatment and control;
- Sample size and statistical power;
- Primary metric;
- Guardrails;
- Duration;
- Seasonality;
- Contamination risk;
- Stopping rule;
- Follow-up period;
- Decision owner.

## Priority experiments

1. Mobile Lite versus Standard entry offer;
2. Mobile Plus feature and price;
3. Monthly recurring versus manual renewal;
4. Prepaid duration;
5. Annual discount;
6. Wallet auto-renewal versus reminder-based renewal;
7. QR versus card-first checkout;
8. Direct versus app-store channel;
9. Telecom or wallet bundle;
10. Family stream and device limits;
11. Extra-member price;
12. Graduated sharing verification;
13. Grace-period and retry timing;
14. Content promotion versus price promotion;
15. Post-promotion price communication.

Test separately among:

- New prospects;
- Existing Standard customers;
- Existing Premium customers;
- Known shared-account users;
- Prepaid lapsed users.

Primary success metric:

\[
\text{Incremental Contribution}
=
\text{Treatment Contribution}
-
\text{Control Contribution}
\]

Measure at least 90 days and preferably 180 days. Annual, content, and sharing tests may require modeled long-term results with confidence intervals.

### Cannibalization test

\[
\text{Incremental Contribution}
=
\text{New-Tier Contribution}
-
\text{Downgrade Loss}
-
\text{Churn Effects}
-
\text{Incremental Cost}
\]

Predefine maximum premium downgrade, blended-contribution decline, and support-cost thresholds before testing.

---

# 14. Risks and mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Entry tier is uneconomic | Rail-specific price floors and contribution modeling |
| Entry tier cannibalizes premium | Existing-customer holdouts and blended-contribution gates |
| Prepaid renewal is weak | Renewal cohorts, reminders, reactivation, duration testing |
| Recurring payment failure | Rail-specific retries, grace periods, alternate rails |
| Platform or partner fees erase margin | Direct web and local-rail strategy; transparent channel pricing |
| Sharing controls cause churn | Graduated verification, travel allowances, appeals, extra members |
| FX weakens USD economics | Constant-currency reporting, local-cost matching, trigger-based repricing |
| USD content costs rise | Local/indexed contracts, treasury scenarios, selective hedging |
| Tax treatment is incorrect | Country tax matrix and accounting signoff |
| Annual plans create refund or FX risk | Separate cash, deferred-revenue, refund, and FX model |
| Fraud and geographic arbitrage increase | Risk scoring, velocity controls, geographic voucher rules, human review |
| Local content fails to retain users | Incremental retained-household tests |
| Complexity increases support costs | Launch caps, entitlement flags, complexity KPIs |
| Customer support is overwhelmed | Capacity gates and fully loaded support cost |
| Partner reporting is unreliable | Contractual audit rights and independent entitlement reconciliation |
| Piracy benefit is overstated | Treat piracy reduction as a measured upside hypothesis |
| Data bundles fail to improve economics | Model data subsidy, usage uplift, legal treatment, and contribution |

---

# 15. Implementation roadmap

## Phase 1: Fact base and economics — 0–90 days

**Owners:** Regional GM, CFO pricing lead, payments lead, tax/legal lead.

- Build country and segment scorecards;
- Validate competitor prices, affordability, data costs, and sharing;
- Confirm tax, payment, privacy, consumer, and content requirements;
- Model each plan and rail in local currency;
- Establish FX, repatriation, and USD-cost scenarios;
- Define the metrics dictionary and event instrumentation;
- Select two to four pilot markets;
- Approve pilot budget and contingency.

**Gate:** validated fact base, legal feasibility, complete unit model, approved budget, and measurement plan.

## Phase 2: Product and operating readiness — 60–150 days

**Owners:** Product, engineering, payments, customer operations.

- Enable local currency and tax-inclusive checkout;
- Integrate two or three priority rails per pilot;
- Build prepaid entitlement and reconciliation;
- Implement retry, grace, refund, and reactivation flows;
- Add device, stream, download, and sharing controls;
- Prepare local-language billing and support;
- Complete fraud, privacy, accounting, and partner tests;
- Validate customer-service capacity.

**Gate:** production billing, collection, refund, support, fraud, reconciliation, and reporting tests pass.

## Phase 3: Controlled pilots — 120–240 days

**Owners:** Growth and regional GM, with finance experiment owner.

- Launch limited products;
- Run pricing, payment, billing-frequency, sharing, and promotion tests;
- Use randomized holdouts;
- Review at 30, 90, and 180 days;
- Track local contribution, cash collection, recognized revenue, and customer outcomes;
- Stop failed variants under predefined criteria.

**Gate:** positive incremental contribution, approved retention and payment performance, no material cannibalization, and no unresolved compliance issue.

## Phase 4: Archetype expansion — 240–365 days

- Extend successful configurations to similar validated markets;
- Renegotiate payment and distribution costs;
- Expand local content only where retention evidence supports it;
- Introduce extra-member monetization where validated;
- Standardize investor reporting;
- Refresh the country classification scorecard.

## Ongoing optimization

- Reforecast monthly;
- Review stable-market prices quarterly;
- Trigger reviews for inflation, FX, tax, or payment-cost changes;
- Reallocate acquisition toward high-LTV cohorts and rails;
- Retire persistently negative or unnecessarily complex plans;
- Refresh legal, tax, privacy, and partner assessments after material changes.

---

# 16. Governance and decision rights

Create a Localization Pricing Council comprising finance, growth, product, payments, tax, legal, content, fraud, customer operations, treasury, and regional leadership.

| Decision | Primary owner | Required control |
|---|---|---|
| Price within approved corridor | Regional commercial lead | Finance approval |
| Material price change | Pricing Council | CFO, tax/legal, regional GM |
| New payment rail | Payments lead | Finance, legal, operations |
| Promotion | Growth lead | Finance and regional approval |
| Negative-margin investment | Executive investment committee | CFO approval |
| Sharing-enforcement change | Product/fraud | Legal and customer-operations approval |
| Local-content investment | Content and regional GM | Finance/investment approval |
| FX-triggered repricing | Treasury/pricing | CFO, legal, regional approval |
| Accounting and tax treatment | Controller/tax | CFO and local adviser signoff |

Escalation rules:

- Legal or privacy issue: legal may pause activity.
- Accounting or tax issue: controller/CFO may block launch.
- Negative contribution: executive investment approval required.
- Entitlement infeasibility: product may block launch.
- Support or billing overload: operations may pause rollout.
- Material partner breach: commercial leadership may suspend entitlement.

Each approval memo should include:

- Target segment and customer problem;
- Price and entitlement;
- Payment rail and channel;
- Expected acquisition, retention, and mix;
- Cannibalization;
- Local contribution;
- CAC and payback;
- Downside FX, tax, churn, and payment cases;
- Customer communications;
- Legal, tax, privacy, accounting, and support readiness;
- Budget and rollback plan.

---

## Final recommendation

Proceed with a **limited, payment-native, prepaid-compatible, household-aware localization program**.

- Use mobile and prepaid access where income, liquidity, or recurring-billing constraints dominate.
- Use Standard and Premium Household products where TV use, family value, and payment maturity support higher monetization.
- Select payment rails by successful collections and retained contribution.
- Treat sharing as a monetization and customer-value issue before treating it solely as leakage.
- Use targeted promotions with holdouts and post-promotion measurement.
- Fund local content against incremental retained households.
- Manage prices and operating economics in local currency.
- Report consolidated USD using a transparent constant-currency and FX bridge.
- Scale only after commercial, customer, operating, and compliance gates are met.

This approach improves affordability and paid penetration without sacrificing financial discipline. It gives operating leaders a practical market-entry model and gives investors a defensible view of contribution, cash collection, accounting revenue, retention, cannibalization, FX exposure, and long-term profitability.

# Multi-Market Green-Bond Financing Strategy

## 1. Recommendation

Establish a **Group Green and Transition Financing Framework** with:

- **Core-green criteria** for projects with strong, measurable environmental benefits;
- a separately governed **transition-finance pool** for credible industrial decarbonization projects that may not satisfy strict green-taxonomy requirements;
- project-level classifications for each relevant taxonomy and market;
- one consolidated proceeds and emissions-data ledger;
- common claims, assurance, governance, correction and reallocation controls;
- country-specific legal, tax, listing and taxonomy supplements.

Do not describe all retrofit expenditure as universally green.

### Recommended financing sequence

1. Build the project, data, governance and proceeds-control platform.
2. Select the first market using an economic, legal, investor-demand and execution scorecard.
3. Prefer a **narrow EUR inaugural green bond** only if the scorecard supports it.
4. Use the **EuGB label only after current EU counsel and a qualified reviewer confirm compliance**. Otherwise issue an ICMA-aligned green bond expressly stated to be **not an EuGB**.
5. Establish a reporting and assurance record.
6. Enter selected Gulf and Asian markets individually.
7. Finance broader or less mature retrofit projects through a separately labelled transition, sustainability-linked, sustainability, project-finance, bank, export-credit or conventional instrument.

A smaller, defensible inaugural issue is preferable to a larger issue supported by weak data or overstated claims.

---

## 2. Financing and instrument model

### Default structure: parent-level issuance

Use a parent-level senior unsecured or otherwise appropriately supported bond, subject to:

- rating and leverage capacity;
- debt-incurrence and negative-pledge restrictions;
- liquidity and refinancing limits;
- tax and accounting treatment;
- intercompany-lending and guarantee capacity;
- foreign-exchange, remittance and convertibility rules.

This structure provides scale, liquidity and centralized allocation. Before funding non-EU subsidiaries, local counsel must confirm that proceeds can legally and economically reach the relevant plants.

Use subsidiary, project or local-currency issuance where local regulation, natural currency matching, tax, investor access or ring-fenced cash flows justify it.

### Instrument decision tree

| Financing need | Appropriate instrument |
|---|---|
| Ring-fenced projects with strong environmental evidence | Green bond |
| Industrial transition projects not meeting strict green criteria | Transition bond or loan |
| Green and social eligible expenditure | Sustainability bond |
| Group-wide, measurable targets where asset allocation is difficult | Sustainability-linked bond |
| Ring-fenced cash flows or project security | Project finance |
| Insufficient evidence for a sustainable label | Conventional debt |

A transition instrument is not an easier green bond. It requires an issuer-level transition plan, interim targets, sector pathways, lock-in controls and transparent residual-emissions treatment.

---

## 3. Project taxonomy and disclosure approach

Assign each project separate statuses:

1. **Core-green eligible under the Group Framework**
2. **EU Taxonomy eligible**
3. **EU Taxonomy aligned**
4. **Local-market green eligible**
5. **Transition eligible**
6. **Excluded**

These statuses must not be conflated. “Core-green eligible under the Group Framework” is an internal classification, not an external global standard.

### Core-green projects

Prioritize, where technically and legally eligible:

- renewable electricity generation;
- qualifying renewable-energy procurement;
- industrial electrification;
- replacement of fossil-fuel equipment with electric alternatives;
- waste-heat recovery;
- industrial energy-efficiency systems;
- renewable-linked storage;
- direct methane, nitrous oxide or other greenhouse-gas abatement;
- qualifying pollution-prevention, water-efficiency or resource-efficiency measures.

### Transition projects

Potential candidates include:

- coal or heavy-fuel-oil substitution;
- process optimization at high-emitting facilities;
- hydrogen systems;
- CCUS;
- biomass or biofuels;
- efficiency upgrades that may extend high-carbon asset life;
- projects with measurable reductions but incomplete taxonomy alignment.

Each transition project must disclose:

- pre- and post-project absolute emissions;
- intensity and production assumptions;
- remaining fossil-fuel use;
- expected asset life;
- lock-in and stranded-asset risk;
- compatibility with future electrification or lower-carbon fuels;
- relevant sector pathway;
- alignment with the Group transition plan;
- lifecycle emissions where material;
- residual emissions;
- reliance on subsidies, offsets or immature technology.

Exclude projects that materially increase unabated fossil capacity, rely on unsupported environmental assumptions, or cannot support a reproducible environmental claim.

### Required project register

For every project record:

- plant, country, legal owner and technology;
- project description, capacity and commissioning date;
- eligible expenditure type;
- environmental objective;
- applicable EU and local taxonomy activity;
- technical criteria, DNSH and safeguards;
- baseline and impact methodology;
- data-quality class;
- new financing, refinancing or reimbursement status;
- additionality and project-readiness assessment;
- co-financing and double-counting analysis;
- reviewer and assurance status;
- concentration exposure by country, technology and entity.

Taxonomy conclusions should distinguish:

1. activity identified;
2. activity eligible for assessment;
3. substantial contribution assessed;
4. DNSH assessed;
5. minimum safeguards assessed;
6. alignment concluded, not concluded or unable to assess;
7. percentage of proceeds aligned.

---

## 4. EuGB approach

Treat the EuGB as a regulated label, not enhanced marketing language.

Pursue it only if:

- the project pool meets applicable EU Taxonomy requirements;
- DNSH and minimum safeguards are evidenced;
- the applicable proceeds-allocation threshold can be met;
- required factsheets, allocation reports and impact reports can be prepared;
- the external reviewer meets applicable EU requirements;
- evidence for non-EU projects supports assessment against the relevant EU criteria.

The commonly referenced framework includes an **85% allocation threshold for relevant proceeds**, subject to applicable flexibility provisions. Current EU counsel and the qualified reviewer must confirm:

- issuer and instrument scope;
- the threshold and flexibility treatment;
- allocation timing and temporary allocation;
- treatment of non-EU projects;
- current technical screening criteria;
- reporting templates;
- reviewer registration and supervision;
- prospectus and liability implications.

No EuGB-oriented marketing should occur before this confirmation.

If EuGB is not supportable, issue an ICMA-aligned green bond and state prominently that it:

- is not an EuGB;
- does not imply full EU Taxonomy alignment;
- contains projects with individually disclosed taxonomy statuses; and
- follows the Group’s stated eligibility and evidence controls.

---

## 5. Emissions-data quality framework

### Data classes

| Class | Evidence profile | Permitted use |
|---|---|---|
| **A — Verified** | Metered or auditable data, stable boundaries, reconciliations and independent verification or assurance | Allocation and quantified impact claims |
| **B — Controlled estimate** | Partial measurement, documented engineering estimates, management review and quantified uncertainty | Allocation only by exception; qualified claims |
| **C — Screening estimate** | Proxies, outdated records, extrapolation or unsupported assumptions | Pipeline screening only |
| **D — Unreliable** | Missing boundaries, unexplained variances or irreproducible calculations | Exclude until remediated |

The inaugural core-green pool should contain only Class A and approved Class B projects. Class C and D projects should not receive initial core-green allocations or support quantified impact claims.

Before issuance, management must set:

- maximum percentage of proceeds for Class B projects;
- maximum percentage of reported impact based on estimates;
- maximum estimation percentage per project;
- permitted exceptions;
- minimum supporting evidence;
- remediation deadline;
- approval authority;
- public disclosure requirements;
- consequences if remediation fails;
- whether Class B projects may support EuGB allocation.

Every plant must have a named data owner, operations approver, finance reviewer, estimation percentage, materiality threshold, remediation date and assurance status.

### Group emissions manual

Adopt one manual covering:

- organizational and operational boundaries;
- subsidiaries, joint ventures, leased and contract operations;
- Scope 1 sources;
- location- and market-based Scope 2;
- emission factors and global-warming-potential values;
- PPAs, RECs and environmental attributes;
- baseline selection and recalculation;
- production, product mix and utilization;
- acquisitions, divestments, outages and process changes;
- biogenic emissions, CCUS, offsets and removals;
- estimation, uncertainty, double counting and restatement.

Report Scope 1, Scope 2 and relevant Scope 3 separately. Do not aggregate them into an unqualified “emissions avoided” figure.

RECs and PPAs are not offsets. Ownership, bundling, geography, timing, retirement and double-counting controls must be documented. Offsets and removals cannot substantiate direct retrofit reductions.

---

## 6. Baselines, additionality and claimability

Use the following baseline hierarchy:

1. measured pre-project baseline;
2. adjusted historical baseline;
3. peer or engineering benchmark;
4. regulatory or market counterfactual only where necessary.

Approve the baseline before commissioning or the first impact period. Do not change it solely to improve reported performance.

Normalize for:

- production and product mix;
- operating hours and utilization;
- feedstock quality;
- weather where relevant;
- maintenance and outages;
- major process changes;
- capacity expansion and production growth.

For each quantified claim disclose:

- baseline and post-project emissions;
- activity data and emission factors;
- project boundary;
- production assumptions;
- uncertainty range;
- ex ante or ex post status;
- gross versus net treatment;
- assurance or verification status.

Prefer **“emissions reduction against an identified baseline”** to “avoided emissions.”

Classify financing as new, refinancing, reimbursement, accelerated, expanded, legally required or already committed. Additionality is a Group credibility control, not automatically a universal legal or ICMA requirement. Do not claim that the bond caused or accelerated a project without evidence.

---

## 7. Proceeds management and assurance

### Proceeds controls

Create a consolidated ledger linked to:

- bond proceeds and bank accounts;
- project budgets, invoices and expenditure dates;
- legal entities and countries;
- currency conversions;
- new-financing and refinancing classifications;
- taxonomy and data classes;
- approvals and assurance evidence.

Controls must include:

1. eligibility-committee approval before allocation;
2. segregation of duties;
3. general-ledger reconciliation;
4. double-counting checks across bonds, loans, grants, subsidies, PPAs, RECs, offsets and other instruments;
5. restrictions on temporary investment;
6. a defined refinancing look-back period;
7. substitution and reallocation rules;
8. quarterly internal monitoring;
9. internal-audit testing;
10. no silent substitution.

The framework must specify whether it permits capex, directly related opex, maintenance, R&D, feasibility work, enabling infrastructure, working capital and refinancing. Local counsel must confirm consistency with each selected label and market.

### Assurance plan

Use separate scopes for:

- Framework alignment;
- EU and local taxonomy analysis;
- project-pool eligibility;
- EuGB review;
- transition classification;
- proceeds allocation;
- impact metrics;
- Shariah review, if relevant.

Use a risk-based matrix:

- assurance over material allocation controls;
- limited assurance for initially immature impact metrics;
- technical verification for material or high-risk baselines;
- plant-level verification for the largest or most emissions-intensive projects;
- stronger review for projects supporting EuGB alignment.

Each engagement must identify the applicable standard, scope, materiality, sampling, reliance on estimates, limitations, qualifications and assurance level. Reviewer independence and conflicts must be documented.

---

## 8. Governance and investor credibility protections

Create a board-approved Green and Transition Finance Committee comprising treasury, finance, sustainability, operations, legal, tax, investor relations, regional subsidiaries, risk and internal audit.

The Committee approves:

- framework and supplements;
- project classifications;
- taxonomy conclusions;
- data exceptions;
- label selection;
- reviewers;
- allocation and impact reports;
- substitutions and reallocations;
- public corrections.

Maintain a central claims register covering:

- offering documents;
- roadshows and oral statements;
- websites;
- annual and sustainability reports;
- social media;
- translations;
- third-party ESG databases;
- index questionnaires;
- bank and distributor materials;
- rating-agency submissions.

Each claim must identify its evidence source, owner, approval date, permitted jurisdictions, review date and status as expected, measured or verified.

Prohibit:

- presenting one taxonomy as equivalent to another;
- calling estimates verified;
- combining Scope 1, 2 and 3 without boundaries;
- using offsets as direct retrofit reductions;
- claiming unsupported additionality;
- using intensity improvements to imply absolute reductions;
- claiming the green label improves credit quality;
- implying that selected projects make the whole Group low-carbon;
- using “net zero,” “carbon neutral,” “zero-emission,” “clean” or “low-carbon” without separate substantiation.

Material errors, ineligibility or underperformance must trigger documented assessment of correction, restatement, reallocation, bondholder notification, reviewer reassessment and regulatory notification where appropriate.

---

## 9. Market sequencing

### Europe

Use EUR first only if the market scorecard demonstrates:

- sufficient investor demand;
- adequate issue size and project pipeline;
- currency fit and acceptable hedging cost;
- credible EU Taxonomy or ICMA positioning;
- manageable legal and assurance costs;
- acceptable rating and refinancing effects.

### Gulf

Assess each jurisdiction separately for:

- issuer and listing eligibility;
- local green or sustainable-bond designation;
- tax, FX and repatriation;
- local disclosure;
- transition-technology treatment;
- investor requirements;
- Shariah review, only if the instrument or marketing requires it.

### Asia

Assess each selected country separately for:

- taxonomy and exchange rules;
- ASEAN applicability;
- transition-finance guidance;
- local-language and verification requirements;
- investor exclusions;
- currency and hedging;
- whether an international EUR/USD issue is preferable to a local bond.

Classify markets as launch, follow-on, opportunistic or no-go.

---

## 10. Implementation roadmap

### First 30 days

- Appoint executive sponsor and Committee.
- Appoint global and local counsel.
- Select framework coordinator and reviewers.
- Inventory projects, debt, capex and currencies.
- Establish preliminary green, transition and excluded pools.
- Identify launch and no-go markets.
- Issue a group-wide claims-control instruction.

### Days 30–90

- Classify plant data A–D.
- Approve the emissions-accounting manual.
- Complete taxonomy, additionality and lock-in assessments.
- Set eligible-expenditure, refinancing and Class B rules.
- Design the proceeds ledger and emissions-data architecture.
- Establish project-readiness and market-selection scorecards.
- Conduct investor, bank and rating-agency sounding.
- Approve the implementation budget.

### Days 90–150

- Finalize the Framework and market supplements.
- Complete the current-law refresh.
- Obtain framework and project-pool reviews.
- Confirm EuGB feasibility.
- Complete local legal, tax, FX, exchange and intercompany analysis.
- Implement data retention, cybersecurity and continuity controls.
- Prepare the investor data room and claims register.
- Obtain Board approval.

### Issuance

- **Rapid ICMA route:** approximately 5–9 months where systems, data and legal readiness are mature.
- **Full-control route:** approximately 9–15 months where EuGB analysis, multiple jurisdictions or significant data remediation is required.

After issuance, monitor allocation monthly, report at least annually, provide event-driven updates for material issues, obtain assurance under the approved matrix and review the Framework before each issuance and at least annually.

---

## 11. Final go/no-go conditions

Do not authorize public marketing or issuance until:

1. each initial project has a documented eligibility conclusion;
2. no Class C or D project is in the core-green pool;
3. Class B use is within approved quantitative limits;
4. baselines and financing classifications are documented;
5. absolute and intensity impacts are separated;
6. Scope boundaries are clear;
7. lock-in risk is assessed;
8. proceeds controls are operational;
9. material metrics have an assurance plan;
10. the selected label is legally and technically supportable;
11. projects are ready to spend and report;
12. the issuer-wide transition narrative is consistent;
13. credit, covenant, tax, FX and intercompany gates are passed;
14. the implementation budget is approved;
15. current local counsel and reviewer confirmations are complete; and
16. the Board approves the Framework, limitations and communications.

---

## Assumptions and matters requiring current confirmation

This strategy assumes the Group can issue at parent level, transfer proceeds across borders and obtain project-level emissions and expenditure evidence. Those assumptions require confirmation.

Before launch, obtain current advice on:

- EuGB scope, criteria, allocation threshold, flexibility, non-EU projects and reviewer requirements;
- EU Taxonomy and sustainability-reporting changes;
- local Gulf and Asian taxonomies, exchanges and disclosure rules;
- eligible expenditure and refinancing treatment;
- local borrowing, guarantees, intercompany loans, remittance and withholding tax;
- FX and convertibility;
- environmental, labour, community, water, biodiversity and pollution obligations;
- PPAs, RECs, offsets and environmental-attribute ownership;
- data protection, confidentiality and cybersecurity;
- listing, settlement, trustee and paying-agent requirements;
- assurance and verification standards;
- bond-document consequences of material errors or loss of eligibility.

**Bottom line:** issue a high-confidence green bond only where the evidence supports the label; maintain a separate transition channel for broader industrial decarbonization; and expand into Gulf and Asian markets only when current law, economics, investor demand, measurement quality and assurance capability support the transaction.

# Multimarket Decarbonization Financing Strategy  
## Executive decision paper — August 2026

## 1. Recommendation

Adopt **one global Green and Transition Finance Framework**, with a separate supplement for each actual transaction. Do not market a single “globally taxonomy-aligned” bond.

### Recommended sequence

1. **Inaugural bond:** issue a parent-level EUR or USD senior unsecured bond under a framework aligned with the **ICMA Green Bond Principles**, using a conservative pool of well-documented retrofit projects.
2. **EU-labelled issuance:** use the EU Green Bond Standard only if the actual issuer, structure, project pool, allocation calculations, reviewer, reporting, and disclosures satisfy the applicable requirements at launch.
3. **Separate transition financing:** finance projects that reduce emissions but do not meet the relevant green criteria through transition bonds, sustainability-linked instruments, green loans, development-bank or export-credit facilities, regional-bank financing, guarantees, or conventional debt with transparent climate reporting.
4. **Regional expansion:** add Gulf or Asian tranches only after selecting specific venues, currencies, investor segments, and project pools.
5. **Data discipline:** treat emissions-data quality as an eligibility condition, not merely a reporting caveat.
6. **Credibility protection:** use central proceeds and claims controls, independent review, appropriately scoped assurance, and prompt correction procedures.

### Preferred first transaction

> A parent-level senior unsecured bond aligned with the ICMA Green Bond Principles, supported by EU and local-taxonomy mapping, and allocated primarily to projects with Grade A or B data.

If the project pool and issuer structure clearly satisfy the EU Green Bond Standard, an EU-labelled tranche may be preferable for European investors. It should not be pursued solely for marketing value.

---

## 2. Status of this recommendation

This is a financing strategy, not a legal opinion, taxonomy opinion, offering document, engineering validation, accounting conclusion, or assurance report.

Before issuance, the group must obtain transaction-specific advice from:

- securities and listing counsel;
- local corporate, tax, foreign-exchange, sanctions, and proceeds-transfer counsel;
- environmental and social advisers;
- engineering and GHG-accounting specialists;
- the proposed external reviewer;
- the assurance provider;
- lead arrangers and rating agencies.

Regulatory and taxonomy conclusions must be refreshed immediately before launch. External review does not replace legal advice; assurance does not guarantee taxonomy alignment; engineering review does not guarantee operating performance.

---

# 3. Framework architecture

## 3.1 Global framework

The Global Green and Transition Finance Framework should establish:

- eligible project categories;
- global exclusions;
- minimum environmental, social, labour, human-rights, and community safeguards;
- project-selection procedures;
- market-by-market taxonomy mapping;
- data-quality requirements;
- management-of-proceeds controls;
- refinancing policy;
- allocation timetable;
- temporary-investment policy;
- impact-reporting methodology;
- external-review and assurance arrangements;
- project-substitution rules;
- taxonomy-change procedures;
- restatement and correction procedures;
- environmental-claims approval controls;
- anti-bribery, sanctions, and conflict-of-interest controls.

The global framework should contain common minimum controls. It should not force every project to satisfy every market’s most restrictive rules.

## 3.2 Transaction supplements

Each supplement should specify:

- issuer, guarantors, ranking, and security;
- currency and tenor;
- governing law;
- listing or distribution route;
- applicable taxonomy or bond standard;
- permitted project pool;
- allocation and impact-reporting obligations;
- refinancing limits;
- external-review scope;
- local disclosure, language, tax, and proceeds-transfer requirements.

A project can qualify under the global framework but be excluded from a particular issuance.

## 3.3 Claims hierarchy

Claims should be controlled in this order:

1. comply with mandatory law and the selected statutory label;
2. satisfy the relevant framework and taxonomy criteria;
3. use only wording supported by the evidence and assurance available;
4. disclose material taxonomy or investor-methodology differences;
5. use conservative wording consistently across written and oral communications.

Investor preferences should inform distribution strategy but should not automatically become global legal requirements.

### Controlled terminology

| Term | Required meaning |
|---|---|
| Taxonomy-eligible | Falls within a taxonomy activity description |
| Taxonomy-aligned | Meets the applicable technical criteria, DNSH, minimum safeguards, and other conditions |
| Taxonomy-mapped | Assessed against a taxonomy, but alignment not established |
| ICMA Green Bond Principles-aligned | Issued under a framework aligned with the voluntary ICMA principles |
| Transition-eligible | Supports a credible transition pathway but is not presented as green |
| Ex post calculated reduction | Calculated after implementation using actual operating data; metered and model-derived components are separately identified |
| Avoided emissions | Reported separately from reductions in the group’s own emissions inventory |

Prohibit, unless specifically substantiated and approved, claims such as:

- “globally taxonomy-aligned”;
- “EU Taxonomy-aligned bond” where only part of proceeds is aligned;
- “net-zero financing”;
- “carbon neutral” based on offsets;
- “Paris-aligned” without a substantiated assessment;
- “verified emissions reductions” where the assurance scope does not support that statement;
- “zero-emission electricity” based solely on certificates.

---

# 4. Project portfolio

## 4.1 Core green pool

The inaugural bond should use projects with:

- defined capex;
- clear climate benefits;
- documented baselines;
- reliable operating data;
- completed environmental and social screening;
- no unresolved material controversy.

Potential projects include:

- industrial electrification;
- energy-efficiency retrofits;
- efficient motors, drives, compressors, boilers, and process equipment;
- waste-heat recovery;
- renewable generation and storage;
- qualifying renewable-power procurement;
- energy-management and sub-metering systems;
- process optimization with measured energy savings;
- enabling infrastructure for low-carbon operations.

For each project, record separately:

- global framework status;
- ICMA category;
- EU Taxonomy eligibility and alignment;
- status under each selected local taxonomy;
- data grade;
- permitted issuance pools;
- expected and ex post calculated impact;
- assurance status.

“Core green” under the group framework does not mean EU Taxonomy-aligned.

## 4.2 Taxonomy-sensitive projects

Hydrogen, biomass, alternative fuels, carbon capture, advanced recycling, and complex process changes require project-specific review covering:

- lifecycle emissions;
- electricity and feedstock sources;
- upstream emissions and methane leakage;
- permanence and leakage for carbon capture;
- water, biodiversity, pollution, labour, and community impacts;
- baseline and counterfactual;
- applicable technical-screening criteria;
- regulatory compliance.

Do not include these projects merely to increase bond size.

## 4.3 Transition pool

Projects that support decarbonization but fail the relevant green criteria should normally be financed separately, including:

- interim fuel switching;
- electrification using a materially carbon-intensive grid;
- efficiency measures below an applicable taxonomy threshold;
- enabling equipment without immediate qualifying reductions;
- projects with material unresolved data gaps.

A transition instrument should include:

- a credible group-wide transition plan;
- material emissions coverage;
- a defined baseline year;
- interim targets and milestones;
- capex linkage;
- a supportable emissions trajectory;
- treatment of residual emissions;
- annual reporting and verification;
- safeguards against inconsistent expansion of unabated high-carbon capacity.

Transition finance must not be a residual category for poorly documented projects.

## 4.4 Exclusions

Absent an exceptional, independently validated case, exclude:

- new unabated coal capacity;
- routine maintenance presented as decarbonization;
- general corporate purposes;
- production expansion without material emissions benefits;
- offsets used to create operational carbon-neutrality claims;
- projects with unresolved serious environmental, labour, human-rights, biodiversity, pollution, or community risks;
- projects lacking evidence for the claims proposed.

---

# 5. Issuer and structural strategy

## 5.1 Parent-level issuance

The default should be a senior unsecured bond issued by the parent or an established finance subsidiary, with proceeds advanced to operating entities through documented intercompany arrangements.

The documents must clearly state that:

- bondholders have recourse to the issuer, not individual plants;
- proceeds are subject to a use-of-proceeds commitment but are not necessarily legally segregated;
- bondholders do not own or hold security over allocated projects;
- subsidiaries may receive proceeds through intercompany loans;
- investors remain exposed to group-wide credit, operating, currency, liquidity, and refinancing risk.

Use “designated for allocation” or “allocated in accordance with the framework,” not “ring-fenced,” unless legal segregation actually exists.

## 5.2 Project or subsidiary financing

Use project-level, subsidiary, or asset-backed financing where:

- revenues are contracted;
- project cash flows are stable;
- local guarantees, subsidies, or export-credit support are available;
- project-finance pricing is materially better;
- local-currency funding is required;
- proceeds cannot efficiently be transferred from the parent.

Consider:

- green loans;
- local-bank facilities;
- equipment or vendor finance;
- energy-service-company/shared-savings structures;
- development-bank financing;
- export-credit support;
- guarantees or political-risk insurance;
- blended finance.

These structures may introduce structural subordination, security-perfection, tax, transfer, and reporting complexity.

## 5.3 Green sukuk

Assess green sukuk only if Gulf demand or pricing justifies the additional complexity. Confirm:

- asset ownership or usufruct;
- Shariah-compliant cash flows;
- bankruptcy remoteness;
- tax and transfer costs;
- treatment of operating or intangible assets;
- local listing and investor rules;
- suitability of a diversified multinational project pool.

---

# 6. Market-specific strategy

## 6.1 European Union

### Two possible routes

**EU Green Bond Standard route:** use only if the issuer, structure, project pool, allocation calculations, reviewer, reporting, and disclosures satisfy the applicable EU requirements.

The group should generally expect the EU regime’s requirement that at least **85% of proceeds over the bond’s life** be allocated to EU Taxonomy-aligned economic activities, with a permitted flexibility component and other conditions. This is a general statutory principle, not a complete compliance test. Counsel and the external reviewer must confirm:

- the calculation base;
- treatment of refinancing;
- flexibility conditions;
- activities not yet covered by the Taxonomy;
- allocation timing;
- changes to technical criteria;
- issuer and listing requirements;
- reviewer status;
- reporting templates.

**ICMA-aligned route:** use where the pool is broader or evidence is incomplete. State expressly that the bond is not issued under the EU Green Bond Standard.

The EU disclosure annex should show, by project or portfolio:

- ICMA category;
- EU Taxonomy eligibility;
- EU Taxonomy alignment;
- amount and percentage of proceeds;
- reasons for non-alignment;
- data limitations;
- remediation plan;
- assurance status.

### EU investor focus

Expect scrutiny of:

- eligibility versus alignment;
- technical-screening evidence;
- DNSH and minimum safeguards;
- Scope 1, Scope 2, and material Scope 3;
- baselines and additionality;
- renewable-energy claims;
- absolute versus intensity reductions;
- consistency with group-wide capex and transition plans;
- continued high-emitting operations elsewhere in the group;
- external-review and assurance scope.

EU investor fund classifications and preferences should be distinguished from direct issuer obligations.

## 6.2 Gulf

The group should initially treat the Gulf as a distribution region and potential financing channel, not as one taxonomy market.

Management should select:

- a preferred route; and
- a fallback route.

For each route, document:

- regulator and exchange;
- foreign-issuer eligibility;
- public-offering versus private-placement rules;
- sustainable-finance guidance;
- external-review requirements;
- continuing disclosure;
- language;
- tax and withholding;
- settlement;
- investor qualification;
- Shariah considerations;
- expected demand and minimum efficient size.

The initial approach will generally be international EUR or USD distribution under the global framework, unless a local listing, local-currency issue, or green sukuk offers a demonstrable advantage.

Do not equate alignment with a national climate programme with EU Taxonomy alignment. Test demand separately with banks, sovereign-related investors, asset managers, family offices, and other relevant investors.

## 6.3 Asia

Management should select no more than two initial Asian markets. A generic “Asian tranche” is not executable.

Score each market on:

- project-taxonomy overlap;
- investor demand;
- public versus private issuance feasibility;
- currency and hedge cost;
- withholding tax;
- language and disclosure burden;
- external-review requirements;
- listing and settlement;
- proceeds transfer and repatriation;
- minimum efficient issue size;
- execution time;
- local bank and development-finance alternatives.

Possible routes are:

1. local-taxonomy-only allocation;
2. ICMA-aligned issuance with explicit local mapping and no local-alignment claim;
3. transition or sustainability-linked financing.

A local-currency issuance should proceed only where natural hedging or efficient hedging exists.

## 6.4 Plant-country execution

A project should not count toward bond-size capacity until the group confirms, for its North African and Southeast Asian location:

- intercompany-lending permissions;
- foreign-exchange approvals;
- capital controls;
- withholding and transfer pricing;
- local permits;
- grant and subsidy interaction;
- cash-pooling restrictions;
- proceeds-transfer route;
- record retention and assurance access;
- local environmental and social requirements;
- local-bank, development-finance, guarantee, or export-credit alternatives.

