## 17. Metrics and data governance

Global People will review policy data at least quarterly using a controlled data dictionary.

Each metric must identify:

- definition;
- source;
- denominator;
- reporting period;
- limitations;
- permitted users;
- retention period;
- whether it is globally comparable, locally comparable, exploratory, or unsuitable for cross-country comparison.

### Business and operational measures

- customer satisfaction;
- service-level attainment;
- delivery predictability;
- quality and rework;
- incident response;
- missed handoffs;
- coverage failures;
- office use against stated purposes;
- arrangement reversals.

### Working-time and wellbeing measures

- overtime;
- reported out-of-hours work;
- missed breaks or rest concerns where measurable;
- workload and fatigue;
- disconnect complaints;
- sick leave;
- engagement, belonging, and burnout;
- retention and regretted attrition.

### Fairness measures

- request approval, denial, and appeal rates;
- denial reasons;
- performance ratings;
- promotions and succession;
- compensation;
- recognition;
- stretch assignments;
- mentoring and sponsorship;
- senior-leader access;
- involuntary exits.

Sensitive-characteristic reporting may occur only where legally permitted and necessary. It requires:

- a documented lawful basis;
- employee notice;
- purpose limitation;
- access controls;
- retention limits;
- minimum cell sizes;
- suppression and aggregation rules;
- re-identification controls;
- required consultation.

Managers may not receive identifiable sensitive-characteristic data unless strictly necessary.

Flexibility requests, approved patterns, appeals, and disconnect complaints must not be used to infer ambition, loyalty, reliability, or promotion readiness.

A material disparity, recurring overtime pattern, or repeated manager breach must have a named owner, remediation plan, target completion date, and follow-up measurement.

